(`k4_exchangeAtS_own_of_join`). `LookNuAt` is had with the data's own mask row as the reading's
masks; the tie on the mask row is spent below the lookup's place's number of slots alone, where it
is a theorem (`maskCut_head`). Asked at every number that tie fails at a run whose pad at the row's
last place is not 0 (Law/W/ReadersHead.lean's head). The proof is one assembly over
`k4_exchangeAtS_own_of_join` (Law/W/FootJoin.lean).

  `k4_exchange_tenth`   **`ExchangeAtS T Said k (Item.formsT m 3 "own")`.**

Not shown here. h: the library's hypothesis `P5J.AtMgA` (Johnson/P5J/MgAtAll.lean): a proposition on
the public files Z, the public part Zp and the first chain cF. WHAT STAYS, BY NAME. Lh (`LaidK4`,
Law/SeatedHead.lean). hRdIs: the thread's parameter Rd is the sentence of the frame's own list
`csHead`: a laying, which a caller pays by rfl. hQ, hx: names of public values. half, fits: on the
prover's list of halves and on a public test (Law/SeatedHead.lean). hb₀, hKp: on the thread's own
parameters. hL (`LookIsK3`, Law/LookPtsHead.lean): the parameter `look` agrees with the text
`lookOfK3` in three fields: `k4LookAt_head` (Law/LookAtHead.lean) has it at the text `k4LookAt`
(Honest/K4/Look.lean); it is not called here. WHEREVER `ThrS` STANDS: hMask (`K4MaskTieAt`,
Law/ScriptAt.lean): the data's mask row against the sender's coins, all but the last; that the
entries so asked lie below the row's free entries is `maskEntries_below_head`
(Law/W/MaskBelowHead.lean), under h, Lh, hQ and the thread; it is not called here; whoever shows
hMask needs it · hA (`MatsAre`): an equation of functions on integers: it binds the list to 0
outside every size; `matsAre_head` (Law/W/MatsAreHead.lean) has it at every witness, in every group;
it is not called here · hc (`CellIsList`, Law/W/CellsTieOf.lean): the honest cells against that
list: it forces that list to stand still inside the sizes, off the matrix of the planes · hiW: the
Script's cells hold an index (`Cm.Idx`: a digit matrix that meets the library's `WFW`: so a clause
on the input), and in the first group the embedding that the library's `embR`
(Inst/Model/FF/Trace.lean) reads off the weights of the honest side's reader is the one that it
reads off the images of that index's matrix. WHAT IS LEFT OF IT: at each token below V and each
coordinate below d one equation of the two sums over the digits at the places that the layout gives
the embedding's unit; no other row or column is asked, so what a reader answers outside these places
cannot make it fail. It ties the reader `weights` to the index that the Script's cells hold: where
`weights` is the identity it holds if the index's matrix bears, at these places, the input's own
entries: whoever lays the Script's cells owes that; no input is shown here to meet it;
`hiW_of_index` (Law/W/IndexHiW.lean) has it from one hypothesis on the weights · hTokP
(`TokensArePrompt`, Law/W/TokPublic.lean), in the first group: a sentence on the public instance;
Law/W/TokOfBytes.lean has it at the lot's own reader where the instance's request bytes decode; that
they decode at a run's instance is not shown here: of what decoding asks, the verifier's test on the
request's words has all but that the file is whole words: `hTokP_of_fine` (Law/W/TokOfFine.lean) has
hTokP at the lot's own reader `k4Tokens` from these two public clauses. BEHIND THE STEP: hJ
